Why do We need concept of service? 
> K8s supports auto healing that is implemented by deployment/replication Controller. If a container goes down, and comes up, we get new Ip. Whereas, service uses labels and sectors so it provides  & support service discovery mechanism. In that case, even if the pod's ip changes changes, it will still serve traffic. 
> Service gives loadbalancing capability. 
> Service Expose pod/deployment to external world


##############################
		Why Service? 
##############################
A developer with create pod / deploy. Assume we have requirement of creating 3 replicas of pod and 10 concurrent users [ 10 users trying to access the pod at the same time ] to manage load balance , we need more replicas.

If a pod goes down, because of k8s auto healing capability another pod will come up because of replicaset. 

When the pod comes up , the ip changes . Pods are ephirmal and they are assigned dynamic ips. If no service is present, users need to access the pods / application using ip address of the pods , it is difficult . Moreover, if a pod goes down and comes up , the ip changes 

Services connect to the pods / Application using labels & selectors 

Deployment - Service - users need to access the service. Service act as load balancer.

Auto Healing would have failed without presence of service.

3 Advantages of Services, It  solves the below requirements -  
1. Service does load balancing by managing load on the pods of the deployment. 
2. Service keeps track of deployment using labels & selectors instead of IPs, it solves service discovery.
3. Service Can expose to external world.  


Types of Kubernetes Services

🔸 ClusterIP
	• Default Kubernetes service type
	• Exposes the service only inside the cluster
	• Assigned a virtual internal IP for internal communication
	• Not accessible from outside the cluster
	• Ideal for internal communication between microservices
🔸 NodePort
	• Exposes the service externally
	• Creates a ClusterIP behind the schene
	• Opens a port on every node in the cluster [  port ranges  30000–32767 ] 
	• Accessible via: http://<NodeIP>:<NodePort>
	• Used when you need simple, direct external access
	• Acts as a foundation for LoadBalancer services


With a NodePort service, there are three key ports to consider:
Target Port: The port on the Pod where the application listens (e.g., 80).
Port: The virtual port on the service within the cluster.
NodePort: The external port on the Kubernetes node (by default in the range 30000–32767).

🔸 LoadBalancer
	• Creates both a ClusterIP and NodePort behind the scenes
	• Then provisions a cloud provider’s external load balancer
	• Provides a single, public IP for the service
	• Ideal for production workloads where you need stable external access
	• Common in cloud environments like AWS, GCP, Azure
	• When we load balancer svc, the LB tells AWS to provide public IP, that is performed by cloud control manager 

🔸 Ingress
	• Not a service type — it's a smart routing layer (reverse proxy)
	• Requires an Ingress Controller (Nginx, Traefik, HAProxy, AWS ALB, etc.)
	• Routes traffic based on:
		• Hostnames (e.g., api.example.com)
		• Paths (e.g., /user, /dashboard)
	• Reduces the need for multiple load balancers
→ One external IP → many internal services
	• Supports TLS/HTTPS, URL rewriting, and advanced routing rules


Init Containers
A Pod can have multiple containers running apps within it, but it can also have one or more init containers, which are run before the app containers are started.
Init containers are exactly like regular containers, except:
	• Init containers always run to completion.
	• Each init container must complete successfully before the next one starts.
